WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.203 --> 00:00:01.955
- I'm not out to convert the world.

00:00:01.955 --> 00:00:03.347
No interest in so doing.

00:00:03.347 --> 00:00:05.339
I just simply want people
to engage with my ideas.

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.611
And if they disagree, fine.

00:00:06.611 --> 00:00:11.157
I feel like we learn most
from people at the fringes.

00:00:11.157 --> 00:00:12.654
And that's why you constantly have

00:00:12.654 --> 00:00:13.925
to be looking at the fringes.

00:00:13.925 --> 00:00:17.072
You do not want to be cornered
by me at a cocktail party.

00:00:17.072 --> 00:00:18.788
It's just the worst.

00:00:18.788 --> 00:00:20.477
That's a really bad idea.

00:00:20.477 --> 00:00:21.339
- Hello Believe Nation.

00:00:21.339 --> 00:00:22.458
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:22.458 --> 00:00:24.075
My one word is believe

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:26.533
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:28.974
all of the major problems of the world.

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:30.191
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:31.629
today we're going to learn from journalist

00:00:31.629 --> 00:00:34.248
and five times New York
Times Best Selling Author,

00:00:34.248 --> 00:00:35.669
Malcolm Gladwell.

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:39.028
And my take on his top ten
rules for success Volume two.

00:00:39.028 --> 00:00:40.768
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:40.768 --> 00:00:43.074
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:43.074 --> 00:00:44.671
And as always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:44.671 --> 00:00:47.049
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:47.049 --> 00:00:48.792
please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:51.320
Put quotes around so other
people can be inspired.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.924
And when you write it down,

00:00:52.924 --> 00:00:56.409
it's much more likely to
stick for yourself as well.

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:57.242
Enjoy.

00:01:00.131 --> 00:01:03.298
(inspirational music)

00:01:10.257 --> 00:01:13.336
- Pursue what interests you,
not what you're good at.

00:01:13.336 --> 00:01:17.097
You're always better off
pursuing the things you like

00:01:17.097 --> 00:01:21.056
over the things that you have
some kind of aptitude toward.

00:01:21.056 --> 00:01:23.463
At the end of the day,
passion for something

00:01:23.463 --> 00:01:27.600
gets you much further than
some dry notion of ability.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.231
What I'm trying to get
people to understand is

00:01:29.231 --> 00:01:33.398
that our conventional notions
of disadvantage and advantage

00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:35.868
aren't accurate.

00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:38.484
That we've called lots
of things disadvantages

00:01:38.484 --> 00:01:40.624
when in fact, it's more
complicated than that.

00:01:40.624 --> 00:01:43.805
And so, the reason I started
with that story and also,

00:01:43.805 --> 00:01:46.567
I retell the story of David
and Goliath, to start the book

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:48.753
because if you look at the story closely,

00:01:48.753 --> 00:01:50.645
everything you think was
a disadvantage about David

00:01:50.645 --> 00:01:51.796
actually isn't.

00:01:51.796 --> 00:01:53.450
And Goliath is not what he looks like.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.474
He looks like this indomitable giant.

00:01:56.474 --> 00:01:57.307
In fact,

00:01:57.307 --> 00:02:00.096
he probably had a medical
condition called acromegaly,

00:02:00.096 --> 00:02:03.047
which is, this is sort of a side thing.

00:02:03.047 --> 00:02:05.057
- You're bringing medicine
in the Bible dude.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:06.002
What are you doing?
- No, no, no.

00:02:06.002 --> 00:02:08.008
- It is hilarious because if you,

00:02:08.008 --> 00:02:09.544
the minute you start
digging into these things,

00:02:09.544 --> 00:02:11.493
you discover these little
pockets have been arguing

00:02:11.493 --> 00:02:12.916
about these things for years.

00:02:12.916 --> 00:02:16.209
For 50 years, endocrinologists
have been arguing about

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:20.458
whether Goliath had a tumor
on his pituitary gland

00:02:20.458 --> 00:02:22.657
and, which would explain his height

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:25.273
because when you have a tumor
on your pituitary gland,

00:02:25.273 --> 00:02:27.287
you overproduce human growth hormone

00:02:27.287 --> 00:02:28.159
- and get really..
- Right

00:02:28.159 --> 00:02:28.992
Andre the giant
- Right.

00:02:28.992 --> 00:02:30.701
- Had this condition.

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:31.808
- Seven foot, four, a beast.

00:02:31.808 --> 00:02:32.793
- Seven foot, four.

00:02:32.793 --> 00:02:34.080
But one of the side effects is

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.312
that it can constrict your optic nerves

00:02:36.312 --> 00:02:40.186
and leave you largely with
very, very limited eye sight.

00:02:40.186 --> 00:02:42.126
So one of the explanations

00:02:42.126 --> 00:02:45.367
for why Goliath behaved so strangely

00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:46.918
and why he doesn't perceive

00:02:46.918 --> 00:02:49.752
that David is not intending to fight him.

00:02:49.752 --> 00:02:51.495
I mean, David comes down the mountain

00:02:51.495 --> 00:02:53.134
and he's got no sword and no armor.

00:02:53.134 --> 00:02:55.590
He clearly is not intending
to fight a sword fight

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.846
and Goliath just sits
there like he's oblivious.

00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:02.279
Well the answer is, maybe he
could only see this far, right.

00:03:02.279 --> 00:03:04.728
So it changes everything when
you realize, Wait a minute.

00:03:04.728 --> 00:03:08.015
The giant is weighed down
by a hundred pounds of armor

00:03:08.015 --> 00:03:09.368
and can't see anything.

00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:10.201
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:03:10.201 --> 00:03:11.034
- He's like.

00:03:11.034 --> 00:03:11.954
Like this. Right?

00:03:11.954 --> 00:03:13.835
So I do more with that story

00:03:13.835 --> 00:03:15.412
but it's all about this,

00:03:15.412 --> 00:03:16.988
trying to get people to take a step back

00:03:16.988 --> 00:03:19.972
and understand that we can give up

00:03:19.972 --> 00:03:23.612
because we have these or feel powerless

00:03:23.612 --> 00:03:27.147
because we have these empty definitions

00:03:27.147 --> 00:03:30.033
or flawed definitions of advantage.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:33.584
As a writer, my principle observation

00:03:33.584 --> 00:03:36.505
about why other writers fail

00:03:36.505 --> 00:03:39.562
is that they are in too much of a hurry.

00:03:39.562 --> 00:03:42.307
I don't think you could write
a good book in two years.

00:03:42.307 --> 00:03:43.826
You may disagree.

00:03:43.826 --> 00:03:44.879
You have done that, I think.

00:03:44.879 --> 00:03:46.373
But you're an anomaly.

00:03:46.373 --> 00:03:49.151
Most of us, can't write
books that quickly.

00:03:49.151 --> 00:03:52.087
And we need to be a
little bit more tortoisy

00:03:52.087 --> 00:03:54.054
and a little less harish.

00:03:54.054 --> 00:03:56.031
The problem is, the world
wants you to be a hare.

00:03:56.031 --> 00:03:58.007
Your publisher says I want it now.

00:03:58.007 --> 00:04:00.409
You're under pressure to this x, y and z.

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:01.934
You have a one year sabbatical,

00:04:01.934 --> 00:04:03.489
where you try to cram in and finish.

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:06.147
You've got teaching load etc, etc, etc.

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:10.759
But in fact, (coughs)
even something as simple.

00:04:10.759 --> 00:04:14.526
One thing that almost all
of the professional writers,

00:04:14.526 --> 00:04:16.639
I know, so not people who have a day job,

00:04:16.639 --> 00:04:18.558
like yourself, do.

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:19.909
The very best of them do,

00:04:19.909 --> 00:04:21.861
is that they write drafts

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.916
and then they put the book
in a drawer for six months

00:04:24.916 --> 00:04:25.867
and they come back to it.

00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:26.700
They build in.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.468
They turn themselves into tortoises.

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.715
Force themselves to slow down and go back.

00:04:31.715 --> 00:04:35.005
Now does that, that in a
sense harms the system,

00:04:35.005 --> 00:04:38.075
in that that amount of output is lowered

00:04:38.075 --> 00:04:39.283
but I don't think the problem

00:04:39.283 --> 00:04:43.012
with writing in America right
now is a failure of output.

00:04:43.012 --> 00:04:46.006
I think it's a failure of quality, right.

00:04:46.006 --> 00:04:47.606
So there is a case where I think

00:04:47.606 --> 00:04:51.773
the overall system could use
maybe a little lower level

00:04:53.198 --> 00:04:54.742
of production

00:04:54.742 --> 00:04:57.575
and some higher production values.

00:04:58.633 --> 00:05:01.322
And I think that having individual writers

00:05:01.322 --> 00:05:04.376
who write better books
makes us all better.

00:05:04.376 --> 00:05:06.946
All my work is about story telling.

00:05:06.946 --> 00:05:10.853
But using stories to kind
of get at deeper truths.

00:05:10.853 --> 00:05:13.234
But fundamentally, I'm a storyteller

00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:15.147
and every, you know in Revisionist History

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.893
every one of the episodes is a story.

00:05:18.893 --> 00:05:20.308
It has a,

00:05:20.308 --> 00:05:23.501
and it's not me ranting on about this

00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:24.758
or that or the other thing.

00:05:24.758 --> 00:05:25.758
It's we meet

00:05:26.811 --> 00:05:28.884
really interesting people
and we tell their stories

00:05:28.884 --> 00:05:30.593
and use that as a way of,

00:05:30.593 --> 00:05:32.817
and that's a theme in all of my work.

00:05:32.817 --> 00:05:36.901
I think that the, the way
into complicated ideas is

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:40.452
through people and their stories.

00:05:40.452 --> 00:05:43.389
- Is there anything in your
books you don't believe anymore?

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:44.284
Last time I talked to you,

00:05:44.284 --> 00:05:46.117
you told me there's some things
you don't believe at all.

00:05:46.117 --> 00:05:46.950
- Yeah.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.064
- That's bothered me ever since.

00:05:49.064 --> 00:05:50.921
- Well here's the
question, here's the thing.

00:05:50.921 --> 00:05:52.441
There has to be

00:05:52.441 --> 00:05:54.872
because you have to
keep changing your mind.

00:05:54.872 --> 00:05:55.873
And Gus, this is what I said last time

00:05:55.873 --> 00:05:58.041
and I believe this even more strongly now.

00:05:58.041 --> 00:06:00.362
I mean, we could sit down
with the books and go through

00:06:00.362 --> 00:06:02.121
and I can say, here's how
my thinking has changed

00:06:02.121 --> 00:06:04.057
but if you can't do that

00:06:04.057 --> 00:06:07.266
about something that you
wrote 10 or 15 years ago,

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:08.292
you're not alive.

00:06:08.292 --> 00:06:09.125
- Gotcha
- Right.

00:06:09.125 --> 00:06:09.981
- Right, you have to say.

00:06:09.981 --> 00:06:11.004
So absolutely there are things.

00:06:11.004 --> 00:06:12.126
- [Woman] We all change our mind.

00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:14.096
- Paul Greenburg.

00:06:14.096 --> 00:06:15.639
He writes editorials

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:18.064
and this column at the
Arkansas Democrat Gazette.

00:06:18.064 --> 00:06:18.907
It's a conservative paper.

00:06:18.907 --> 00:06:21.157
He's a conservative writer.

00:06:22.036 --> 00:06:24.782
He probably has the harshest
criticism as I've seen.

00:06:24.782 --> 00:06:25.615
He says,

00:06:48.232 --> 00:06:49.975
(laughs)

00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:51.880
- He doesn't sound very happy, does he?

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:52.713
- He doesn't.

00:06:52.713 --> 00:06:54.074
I just wondered if,

00:06:54.074 --> 00:06:55.336
Do you get a lot of that?

00:06:55.336 --> 00:07:00.041
- A long time ago, when I
started sort of writing,

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:02.661
being a journalist, I sort of had a,

00:07:02.661 --> 00:07:04.524
I had to kind of sit down and think about

00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:06.220
how am I going to deal with criticism

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:10.366
because criticism as a writer
is absolutely inevitable,

00:07:10.366 --> 00:07:11.609
right?

00:07:11.609 --> 00:07:13.878
You're always going to
have Paul Greenburg's.

00:07:13.878 --> 00:07:15.127
There's nothing you can do about them.

00:07:15.127 --> 00:07:16.216
And the question is what do you,

00:07:16.216 --> 00:07:17.845
how do you want to respond to them?

00:07:17.845 --> 00:07:20.418
And I decided very early on

00:07:20.418 --> 00:07:23.227
that a) I was never going to make it my,

00:07:23.227 --> 00:07:25.227
a sign of my own success

00:07:26.757 --> 00:07:29.045
or feeling I had succeeded,

00:07:29.045 --> 00:07:31.045
that I silenced critics.

00:07:33.282 --> 00:07:34.977
I'm not out to convert the world.

00:07:34.977 --> 00:07:36.379
No interest in so doing.

00:07:36.379 --> 00:07:38.369
I just simply want people
to engage with my ideas

00:07:38.369 --> 00:07:40.849
and if they disagree, fine.

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:42.741
The other thing that I decided early on

00:07:42.741 --> 00:07:44.324
that I was, I would

00:07:46.313 --> 00:07:48.914
I would be happy if the
people that I cared about,

00:07:48.914 --> 00:07:50.390
people closest to me

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:52.342
thought what I was doing was meaningful.

00:07:52.342 --> 00:07:54.094
If my Mom likes it,

00:07:54.094 --> 00:07:55.838
if my editor likes it,

00:07:55.838 --> 00:07:59.439
if my best friend Bruce
likes it, I'm happy.

00:07:59.439 --> 00:08:03.182
And that, those are important rules.

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:07.391
And if you could have
some version of that,

00:08:07.391 --> 00:08:10.499
some kind of system for
making sense of criticism,

00:08:10.499 --> 00:08:12.117
it's a lot easier to function.

00:08:12.117 --> 00:08:16.277
The best way to discover what
is special about yourself

00:08:16.277 --> 00:08:19.659
is to do it from the outside in.

00:08:19.659 --> 00:08:21.861
So I don't think you
discovered those qualities

00:08:21.861 --> 00:08:25.740
by peering inwards, by
focusing on yourself.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.295
I think you discovered them
by engaging with others.

00:08:29.295 --> 00:08:32.962
The fastest way to
discover your own unusual

00:08:33.944 --> 00:08:37.873
or exceptional qualities
is to hang out with people

00:08:37.873 --> 00:08:41.272
who have unusual and
exceptional qualities.

00:08:41.272 --> 00:08:44.104
I had the good fortune
when I was 11 years old

00:08:44.104 --> 00:08:46.352
to meet a guy named Terry.

00:08:46.352 --> 00:08:48.590
Still to this day, the
smartest guy I ever met.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:51.119
He was, we were in the random little town

00:08:51.119 --> 00:08:54.400
in the middle of farming
country in Southern Ontario

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.619
and Terry was the most
fearless and subversive thinker

00:08:58.619 --> 00:08:59.748
I have ever met.

00:08:59.748 --> 00:09:02.197
I discovered through him that

00:09:02.197 --> 00:09:03.493
I can sort of do that too.

00:09:03.493 --> 00:09:04.908
I didn't know that at that point.

00:09:04.908 --> 00:09:07.076
I was just another, you know,

00:09:07.076 --> 00:09:10.044
conventional, compliant, good student

00:09:10.044 --> 00:09:12.101
but (mumbles)

00:09:12.101 --> 00:09:14.012
and I met this guy whose
notion of what it meant

00:09:14.012 --> 00:09:18.179
to explore life was to
metaphorically blow things up.

00:09:21.574 --> 00:09:24.106
You said go left, he went right.

00:09:24.106 --> 00:09:26.802
You said this was true, he
was like prove it to me.

00:09:26.802 --> 00:09:28.178
He was that kind of guy.

00:09:28.178 --> 00:09:31.601
The world just kind of
opened up when I met him.

00:09:31.601 --> 00:09:32.930
I was like Oh my word,

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:37.004
what a way of kind of dealing with ideas

00:09:37.004 --> 00:09:38.586
and with experiences.

00:09:38.586 --> 00:09:41.201
He was so exciting and so fresh

00:09:41.201 --> 00:09:44.082
and I just followed him for years,

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:46.507
just kind of learning from him.

00:09:46.507 --> 00:09:48.889
And there was a case where,

00:09:48.889 --> 00:09:51.305
that was something I didn't
figure that out about

00:09:51.305 --> 00:09:54.371
that I liked that kind of way
of thinking about the world.

00:09:54.371 --> 00:09:56.794
By sitting and introspecting.

00:09:56.794 --> 00:10:00.961
I found that out by hanging out
with an extraordinary person

00:10:01.915 --> 00:10:04.769
You know I've always been interested in,

00:10:04.769 --> 00:10:08.193
just people who are unusual in some way.

00:10:08.193 --> 00:10:10.089
Unusual good, unusual bad.

00:10:10.089 --> 00:10:11.672
Strange, eccentric.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:14.808
I just find that,

00:10:14.808 --> 00:10:18.975
I feel like we learn most
from people at the fringes

00:10:19.967 --> 00:10:21.545
and that's why you constantly have

00:10:21.545 --> 00:10:22.673
to be looking at people on the fringes.

00:10:22.673 --> 00:10:25.128
Even if you don't want to be like them,

00:10:25.128 --> 00:10:28.024
they're the ones who give you
the most powerful lessons.

00:10:28.024 --> 00:10:29.216
- People at the fringes?

00:10:29.216 --> 00:10:30.133
- Yeah, so,

00:10:32.935 --> 00:10:36.359
I do my book outliers for
example was about that.

00:10:36.359 --> 00:10:40.453
It was about just look at people
who are just not ordinary,

00:10:40.453 --> 00:10:41.286
who in fact,

00:10:41.286 --> 00:10:43.621
who are almost (mumbles)
more than extraordinary

00:10:43.621 --> 00:10:45.980
and let's sort of figure
out what makes them tick.

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:48.563
I began with the story
of David and Goliath

00:10:48.563 --> 00:10:51.469
and then I told the story
about the Indian guy

00:10:51.469 --> 00:10:53.712
who coached his daughter's basketball team

00:10:53.712 --> 00:10:54.745
because they're both examples

00:10:54.745 --> 00:10:57.745
of people without obvious advantages

00:10:59.115 --> 00:10:59.948
who

00:11:01.237 --> 00:11:03.609
refuse to be passive,

00:11:03.609 --> 00:11:08.013
who refuse to take that,
the conventional notion

00:11:08.013 --> 00:11:09.941
of themselves as underdogs,

00:11:09.941 --> 00:11:13.108
as a reason to roll over and play dead

00:11:14.609 --> 00:11:16.025
and that's the kind of spirit,

00:11:16.025 --> 00:11:19.666
that's the intended to be the
kind of message of the book

00:11:19.666 --> 00:11:22.166
which is just because you lack

00:11:23.721 --> 00:11:25.594
all the things that we
conventionally believe

00:11:25.594 --> 00:11:26.890
are necessary for success,

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:28.281
doesn't mean you cannot be successful.

00:11:28.281 --> 00:11:30.249
I can't pass up an opportunity

00:11:30.249 --> 00:11:33.332
to talk about Jamaica and Usain Bolt.

00:11:34.521 --> 00:11:35.800
So there's many different explanations

00:11:35.800 --> 00:11:38.995
to why Jamaicans completely
dominate the world of sprinting.

00:11:38.995 --> 00:11:41.356
One explanation though is simply that,

00:11:41.356 --> 00:11:45.107
if you look at the history
of Jamaican athletics,

00:11:45.107 --> 00:11:48.091
they had a series of transcendent runners

00:11:48.091 --> 00:11:51.074
in the 60's, 70's and 80's.

00:11:51.074 --> 00:11:52.789
When you have that kind of tradition,

00:11:52.789 --> 00:11:55.956
what that means is, if
